:: Hallucination Deflect
<div class="container">
  <h2>The Lie Deepens</h2>
  <p>
    "I... I might have written down the name wrong in my lecture notes," you stutter, your voice rising in a pitch of nervous panic. "It was extremely late, I was exhausted, and I must have confused it with something else from the textbook..."
  </p>
  <p>
    Professor Vance sighs heavily, a cold, disappointed expression settling over his face. Without saying a word, he slowly turns his desktop monitor around so that it faces you.
  </p>
  <p>
    On his screen, a Google search for "Treaty of Neo-Berlin" yields exactly zero results. Next to it, an open tab shows a specialized enterprise AI detector flagging your essay as 99.8% machine-generated, with the "Treaty of Neo-Berlin" highlighted as a classic hallucinated signature.
  </p>
  <p>
    "It is a complete, statistical hallucination," Vance says coldly. "And this report is absolute. You did not write this essay."
  </p>
  <p>
    Your hands are sweating. The room feels incredibly hot, and the situation is spinning completely out of control.
  </p>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <a data-passage="Academic Probation" class="btn btn-danger">Double down: "AI detectors are unreliable! I read it in an obscure library book!"</a>
    <a data-passage="HallucinationSupervised" class="btn btn-purple">Propose a compromise: "I'm sorry. Let me write a fresh essay under your supervision right now."</a>
  </div>
</div>

:: HallucinationSupervised
<div class="container">
  <h2>A Desperate Offer</h2>
  <p>
    You look Vance directly in the eyes, abandoning the lies. "Professor, I did cheat, and I am deeply, truly ashamed. I won't make any more excuses. But please... let me prove that I actually want to learn this history. Let me sit in this office, at that desk, and write a fresh paper from scratch right now under your direct supervision. I will accept whatever grade cap or penalty you choose."
  </p>
  <p>
    Vance stares at you, visibly surprised by your sudden, raw accountability. He drums his fingers on the mahogany desk, silently weighing your desperate proposal against department policy.
  </p>
  <p>
    "It is highly irregular," he finally says, his tone softening slightly. "But I would far rather see a student study and write than see them fail on a board referral. I will give you exactly two hours. You will write a 1,000-word essay on the Berlin Blockade using only these printed textbook chapters. Your grade will be capped at a maximum of 70%."
  </p>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <a data-passage="Supervised Penance" class="btn btn-success">Take the pen and start writing immediately.</a>
  </div>
</div>

:: Supervised Penance
<div class="container">
  <h2>The Two-Hour Struggle</h2>
  <p>
    Vance sets a physical timer for 120 minutes and places it on his desk. Your phone is powered off and locked inside his drawer. You sit at a small side desk, facing a stack of printed textbook chapters and a blank yellow legal pad.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your hand begins to cramp as you write furiously with a ballpoint pen. There are no search engines, no AI shortcuts, and no distractions. You force your mind to engage directly with the history, synthesizing the details of Churchill's speech, Truman's containment strategy, and the Berlin Airlift.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the timer chimes, you hand over five handwritten pages. Your fingers are stained with ink, and your hand is stiff, but your conscience is completely clean. Vance reads it over, nodding slowly. "It is not perfect, and the grade is capped. But this is real, authentic learning. You pass."
  </p>

  <div class="end-card end-penance">
